in this episode I will show you how to  display and hide a loading spinner when  retrieving a synchronous fire-based data  from an angular application loading  indicators are an important user  interface feature because they show the  end user when more data is incoming step  1 create the spinner component we're  going to create a spinner component that  can be reused throughout the application  to make it look cool I'm going to use  the spin kit CSS library which can just  be copied directly into the components  CSS and HTML files at this point you  don't need to change anything in the  components typescript step to retrieve  asynchronous data we need to load some  data from firebase in order to make use  of the spinner in this example I'm  loading a firebase list observable via  the angular fire to package the actual  data is just an array of items from a  to-do list this code is originally  created for the real-time database video  so check out it if you want to learn  more about asynchronous data streams  from firebase step 3 the show and hide  the spinner we want to show the spinner  in the items list component when the  data is being retrieved  first we define a variable called show  spinner in the component and set it to  true by default during the ng on a net  life cycle so the data is requested from  the service as the firebase lists  observable we can determine if the data  has been loaded by subscribing to it the  function inside the subscribed call will  be executed after the observable emits  some data so we can simply set the show  spinner variable to false once this  happens in the template we can use the  ng-if directive with the show spinner  variable to control the visibility of  the actual spinner component itself  going back to the app we can see the  spinner is visible for a split second  before the data is loaded the longer it  takes for your data to load the more  useful the spinner is going to be for  your end-users that's it for loading  spinners see you next time  [Music]
 

